course: dictionary
lesson: 7662
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word proof is a good example. «A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof.» Here proof means evidence: something that shows a thing is true.
explain: In maths, proof means something different: a set of clear steps that shows a statement must always be true, not just for one example. «Saying someone cheated without proof is unfair.» The clue is the word without: proof is what you need before you can say something happened.
explain: Add -proof to the end of a word and it means protected against something. Waterproof means water cannot get in, and childproof means children cannot easily open it. Ask which meaning fits the sentence, then swap your meaning in and check it still makes sense.
fact: Proof can mean evidence that shows something is true.
fact: In maths, a proof shows why something is always true.
fact: The ending -proof means protected against something, as in «waterproof» or «childproof».
fact: The thylacine, or Tasmanian tiger, was a striped meat-eating marsupial; the last known one died in a zoo in 1936, and despite sightings no proof of any living since has been found.
fact: A sighting is someone saying they saw something, but proof is evidence that others can check.
exercise: «A blurry or faraway photo is not real proof.» In this sentence, what does proof mean? | A small picture | *Evidence that shows something is true | A way of spelling | A kind of camera
exercise: What does «waterproof» mean? | Made of water | *Protected against water | Full of water | Very wet
exercise: «Adverts sometimes spell words wrong on purpose, so a sign is no proof of a spelling.» What does this tell you? | Spelling is not important at all | Adverts are never fun | Every sign is always spelled correctly | *A sign alone does not show that the spelling is correct
test: In maths, what does a proof show? | A guess that might be right | A list of numbers | A picture of a shape | *A statement is always true, with clear steps
test: What could be proof of payment? | A pencil | A puppy | *A receipt | A cloud
exam: A detective looks for proof of who did it. What is the detective looking for? | A quiet room | A map of the town | A new coat | *Evidence that shows who did it
